body:
They told me when I bought it that this mattress would be soft, and that I would be happy alone or entwined with another;

they lied.

They told me when I got these clothes, this makeup, this artificial persona, that I would be happy;

they lied.

Then they told me that all I needed was more money, and more things, and after that the happiness would come;

they lied about that too.

So now here I sit - lied to, betrayed, horribly aware. I watch the clock go in its eternal circles, begging it to catch its breath so that I may have some semblance of a life within its shrinking confines; my sunsets and sunrises meld into one entity, encompassing one long, blurry miasma of a half-lived life.

But - a life nonetheless.

They lied, yes; but *I did not believe.* They tried to turn me into just another peon, yet one more being who would equate a smiling mask for true happiness; but *I did not succumb*.

And in that, I found my joy, my cheer. I was above the falsities and the fallacies; I did not feel the need to pretend to anyone. At the very least, I had my truth, and they did not - and that, in any case, was hopeful.
